  "marks": "Mastering: Sophisticated argument engaging with complexity. Agrees: the scale of the Colosseum (50,000 capacity), the elaborate design (hypogeum, velarium), venationes and midday executions all suggest violence was institutionalised as entertainment. However, challenges the claim: Juvenal and Seneca show educated Romans were critical; gladiators were not always killed; free volunteers chose to fight; the games served political and social functions beyond mere cruelty. Reaches a supported judgement using named sources (Colosseum, Juvenal, Seneca).\nSecure: Addresses both sides with at least two named sources; clear argument and conclusion.\nDeveloping: Some relevant points; evidence limited or one-sided.\nEmerging: Simple description with no judgement."
 }
]

