--- DESCRIPTION ---
Slaves and freedmen in Roman city life — their work, legal status, manumission and social mobility.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Slavery at the Heart of Roman Society</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Roman city life &mdash; its baths, its banquets, its great houses and its public buildings &mdash; depended on the labour of enslaved people. Slavery was not a marginal feature of the Roman world; it was embedded in every level of society. Wealthy households might own hundreds of slaves; a prosperous artisan might own two or three; and the Roman state itself owned slaves who worked in mines, on public building projects and in the administrative offices of the city. Modern scholars estimate that slaves may have made up somewhere between 20 and 35 per cent of the population of Italy during the late Republic and early Imperial period, though precise figures are impossible to establish.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">A person could become a slave in several ways. The most common route in the earlier Republic was capture in war: defeated enemies were sold into slavery in enormous numbers. The children of enslaved women were born enslaved regardless of who their father was. People could also be enslaved as a punishment for certain crimes, or through debt, though debt bondage was abolished relatively early in Rome&rsquo;s history. Exposure of unwanted infants &mdash; a practice that was legal in Rome &mdash; sometimes resulted in children being collected by slave traders and raised for sale.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Cover this and list three ways a person could become a slave in Rome and three types of work enslaved people performed, without looking.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Enslaved people in Roman cities performed an enormous range of tasks: domestic service (cooking, cleaning, childcare, dressing their owner), skilled trades (reading, accounting, medicine, teaching, craft work), public labour (building, roads, mines), and personal entertainment (musicians, readers, secretaries). The range of enslaved people&rsquo;s roles meant that some held positions of considerable responsibility and even influence within wealthy households.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">The Legal Position of Slaves</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">In Roman law, an enslaved person was legally defined as a <dfn class="term" data-def="A thing; in Roman law, enslaved people were classified as property (res) rather than persons, meaning they had no legal standing of their own.">res</dfn> &mdash; a thing, not a person. This meant they could be bought, sold, hired out, punished and even killed by their owner, though the law eventually placed some limits on extreme cruelty. Slaves could not own property, make contracts or appear in court. Any earnings or property a slave accumulated was technically their master&rsquo;s, though in practice many enslaved people were allowed to accumulate a personal fund called the <dfn class="term" data-def="A sum of money or property held by an enslaved person with the informal permission of their owner, which could eventually be used to purchase their own freedom.">peculium</dfn>, which they might use to buy their freedom.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">The relationship between enslaved people and their owners varied enormously. Pliny the Younger, writing in his <em>Letters</em> (3.14), describes being shocked and saddened by the murder of a fellow senator&rsquo;s household slaves, and expresses genuine affection for his own staff. This is worth reading carefully: Pliny is an elite male writing about elites for an elite audience, and his sympathy does not challenge the institution of slavery itself. Petronius&rsquo;s <em>Satyricon</em> presents a different perspective: the nouveau-riche freedman Trimalchio boasts at his dinner party about having once been a slave and having risen to great wealth, but his treatment of the enslaved people at his table reveals the arbitrary cruelty that underpinned even a &lsquo;kind&rsquo; master&rsquo;s power.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Slave Resistance and Roman Anxiety</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8">Romans were aware of the potential for enslaved people to resist or rebel. The slave revolt led by Spartacus (73&ndash;71 BC), in which tens of thousands of enslaved people defeated Roman armies for two years before being crushed, cast a long shadow over Roman attitudes. Pliny (Letters 3.14) reveals this anxiety when he notes that a murdered senator&rsquo;s household slaves knew about the murder in advance but said nothing: the whole household could be executed under Roman law if a master was killed, as a deterrent. This practice &mdash; the collective punishment of all slaves in a household &mdash; shows both Roman anxiety about slave revolt and the legal violence that kept the system in place.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Manumission: The Path to Freedom</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10"><dfn class="term" data-def="The legal act by which an owner freed an enslaved person in Rome, making them a freedman or freedwoman.">Manumission</dfn> &mdash; the freeing of an enslaved person &mdash; was a distinctive feature of the Roman slave system. It was far more common in Rome than in most ancient societies. Owners might free slaves as a reward for long service, as a provision in their will, as an act of affection, or (cynically) to avoid having to support ageing enslaved people who were no longer economically useful. An enslaved person who saved enough from their peculium could also purchase their own freedom. Formal manumission by a Roman citizen created a new legal status: the <dfn class="term" data-def="A formerly enslaved person (man or woman) who had been freed through manumission, holding Roman citizenship but with certain social and legal restrictions.">libertus</dfn> (freedman) or liberta (freedwoman) became a Roman citizen.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n11" data-revision-tip="Explain in your own words: what were the limits on the freedman's status in Roman society? What could he or she not do?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Freed slaves received Roman citizenship but faced significant restrictions. They could not stand for election to the Senate or hold major magistracies, and were expected to perform certain duties for their former owner, now called their <dfn class="term" data-def="The former owner of a freed slave, who became their patron and to whom the freedman owed certain obligations of loyalty and service.">patronus</dfn>. Their children, however, faced no such restrictions and could advance fully in Roman society.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">Freedmen in Roman Commerce and Society</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">Freedmen played a vital role in Roman commerce and administration. Because Roman aristocrats considered direct involvement in trade to be beneath their social status, they frequently ran businesses through freedmen agents who could conduct transactions on their behalf. Many freedmen became wealthy merchants, bankers, craftsmen and contractors. The most famous literary example is Trimalchio in Petronius&rsquo;s <em>Satyricon</em> (the Cena Trimalchionis, chapters 26&ndash;78): a former slave who has accumulated enormous wealth through trade and now hosts an extravagant dinner party, surrounding himself with every luxury money can buy.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n14">Petronius presents Trimalchio as simultaneously admirable and ridiculous &mdash; his wealth is real but his taste is vulgar, his manners are ostentatious, and his constant references to his slave origins suggest someone who has not quite escaped his past. The <em>Satyricon</em> is satirical fiction, so we should not read it as a straightforward historical document. Nevertheless, it tells us a great deal about Roman anxieties around social mobility: the freedman who becomes too rich, too powerful, too visible threatens the established social order.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>What the Tomb of Naevoleia Tyche Tells Us</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n15">The <dfn class="term" data-def="A funerary monument in Pompeii erected by the freedwoman Naevoleia Tyche, decorated with a ship, a bisellium (a ceremonial two-seater chair) and an inscription naming her public benefactions.">Tomb of Naevoleia Tyche</dfn> at Pompeii is a prescribed material source that combines several of these themes. Naevoleia was a freedwoman who erected a large, elaborately decorated tomb for herself and her husband Gaius Munatius Faustus. The inscription records that she paid for public construction projects out of her own money. The carved decoration shows a ship, suggesting commercial wealth, and a bisellium &mdash; a two-seated ceremonial chair given to those who had held civic office or received public honours. A freedwoman funding public buildings and commemorating herself with the symbols of civic prestige is a striking statement about what freedmen and women could achieve, and about how they used material culture to establish their social identity.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n17">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">Slavery was central to Roman city life. Enslaved people were legally classified as property (res), had no legal standing, and performed work ranging from domestic service to skilled trades. Routes into slavery included war captivity, birth to an enslaved mother, and crime.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">Manumission was relatively common in Rome. Freed slaves became liberti (freedmen/women) with Roman citizenship, but faced social restrictions. Their former owner became their patronus, and they owed obligations of loyalty and service.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Petronius&rsquo;s Satyricon (Trimalchio) and Pliny&rsquo;s Letters 3.14, alongside the Tomb of Naevoleia Tyche, are the key sources. Petronius reveals elite anxieties about freedman wealth; the tomb shows how freedpeople used material culture to assert civic identity.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n16">Questions about slaves and freedmen often ask you to use a literary source. The key sources are Pliny (Letters 3.14 &mdash; murder of a senator&rsquo;s slaves) and Petronius (Satyricon, Dinner with Trimalchio &mdash; the freedman as a social-climbing figure). When using Petronius, remember it is satirical fiction: state that it is not straightforward evidence, then explain what the satire reveals about Roman anxieties. The biggest error is to treat Petronius as a realistic account. For material source questions, the Tomb of Naevoleia Tyche is your key example of a freed person&rsquo;s self-presentation. Always push from &lsquo;what it shows&rsquo; to &lsquo;what it reveals&rsquo; about Roman attitudes to social mobility.</p>

