 {
  "text": "Read the following description of a lararium discovered in a Pompeian house:\n\n'Above the kitchen hearth, a shallow niche was set into the plaster wall. Inside, two dancing figures in short tunics faced one another, each holding a drinking horn and a dish. Between them stood a draped figure pouring wine over a flaming altar. Beneath the altar, a large serpent moved towards an egg placed at the base of the painting. On the ledge in front, a small bronze incense-burner showed traces of recent use.'\n\nWhat does this source tell us about religion in Roman daily life? Use details from the source in your answer.",
  "type": "5 marks — Source Analysis",
  "marks": "Mastering: Uses specific details from the source (two dancing Lares with horns and dishes; central figure sacrificing; the protective serpent below; incense burner showing recent use) to build an analysis of how Roman domestic religion worked in practice: the Lares as household protectors, the serpent as a guardian symbol, the regular use of incense as evidence of daily worship. Draws on wider knowledge (lararia across Pompeii, the Penates, the Genius of the paterfamilias) to contextualise. Notes what the source can and cannot tell us.\nSecure: Identifies two details and explains what they reveal about domestic religious practice.\nDeveloping: Identifies one detail with limited explanation.\nEmerging: Describes the source without analysis."
 },
 {
  "text": "Explain how Roman religion was connected to civic and political life in the Roman city.",
  "type": "4 marks — Explain",
  "marks": "1 mark per developed point (up to 4). Points may include: temples stood in the forum at the civic centre, connecting religion to the city's identity (1); Augustus reorganised Rome's neighbourhoods around compital shrines to the Lares and his own Genius, making religion a tool of political loyalty (1); festivals such as the Lupercalia and Saturnalia were public events on the official Roman calendar (1); the founding myth of Rome (Romulus, Aeneas) was linked to divine ancestry, giving religion a role in legitimising power (1); emperors sponsored games and religious festivals to win popular favour (1)."
 },
 {
  "text": "How far do the lararia of Pompeii help us understand religion in Roman daily life? What can they tell us, and what are their limits as evidence?",
  "type": "6 marks — Explain (Extended Response)",
  "marks": "Mastering: Weighs what lararia reveal (evidence of domestic religion in every social class; images of Lares, Penates and Genius; evidence of daily use through incense burners and offerings; range from humble kitchen niches to elaborate marble shrines) against limits (cannot tell us how devout people actually were; the representations are formulaic and may not reflect individual belief; no voice of enslaved people who also worshipped at household shrines). Compares with literary sources (Horace, Pliny) to build a fuller picture. Balanced conclusion.\nSecure: Identifies at least two things lararia tell us AND at least one limit; some reference to literary sources.\nDeveloping: Mostly describes lararia rather than evaluating their usefulness.\nEmerging: Brief answer with little evaluation."
 },
 {
  "text": "'Roman religion was more about social control than genuine belief.' How far do you agree? Use evidence from literary and material sources in your answer.",
  "type": "8 marks — Essay (Extended Response)",
  "marks": "Mastering: Sophisticated argument exploring both interpretations. Supports social control: the Saturnalia uses apparent role reversal to relieve tension while leaving the slave system intact; Augustus weaponised compital shrines for political loyalty; emperors sponsored festivals to manage public opinion. Against: the personal nature of daily lararium worship (individual offerings, home shrines, the incense burner in use) suggests genuine individual devotion; Horace references religious rites naturally, not cynically; Romans genuinely feared divine anger. Reaches a supported judgement drawing on named literary (Horace Satires, Pliny Letters) and material (Pompeian lararia, compital shrines) sources.\nSecure: Both sides argued with named sources; clear judgement.\nDeveloping: Mostly one-sided; limited evidence.\nEmerging: Simple points with no judgement."
 }
]

